1 Introduction

Contemporary discussions about ethical and socially-beneficial AI often refer to two key principles: AI systems should (a) be made explainable to humans, and (b) be trusted by their users. Although recent scholarship has paid much attention to practical questions of how to make AI systems explainable and how to build trust in them, few have stopped to ask: can these two principles be meaningfully pursued together?
To some, this question is a non-starter: these two principles not only can, but must be jointly pursued, for explainability is a key factor for ensuring trust in AI. Indeed, a commonly held view is that AI systems should be made explainable so that we can build trust in these systems, and in turn, so that we can ensure their widespread adoption and acceptance (Chatila et al., 2021; Durán & Formanek, 2018; Deloitte, 2021; European Commission 2019; ISO/IEC, 2020; Stanton & Jensen, 2021). Hereafter, we will call this view the ‘process model’.1 Insofar as we only care about the explainability of AI to the extent that it leads to trust, the process model seems unproblematic. However, as we will discuss, the moral scope of these two principles extends far beyond this particular instrumental connection. Specifically, we care about explainability because an AI system’s explanations might help us pursue a variety of morally desirable goals beyond trust: fairness, accountability, and legitimacy, to name a few. Here is the problem. Even if the process model is correct, it remains to be asked: what happens after we obtain trust and, finally, acceptance? Can explanations still meaningfully serve their expected normative purpose even after trust is established?
In this paper, we suggest that if we were to account for the rich and diverse moral reasons that ground the call for explainable AI, and consider what it means to “trust” AI in a descriptively rich sense of the term, we would uncover a deep and persistent tension between the two principles. The problem, as we shall explain, is that these two principles give rise to incompatible demands about the appropriate attitude that users should adopt towards their AI systems. For the explanations of AI systems to be useful for the various normative ends they are expected to serve, users must adopt a deliberative attitude. However, trust in AI systems entails that users adopt an unquestioning attitude – one that is incompatible with the deliberative attitude.
Uncovering the exact nature of these attitudinal tensions between explainability and trust, however, requires considerable theoretical groundwork. We lay some of this groundwork in Sect. 2, where we explore in greater detail what the popular calls for explainability and trust in AI entail. Section 3 elaborates on the process model, and briefly highlights its shortcomings. Section 4 follows by making explicit the attitudinal tensions between explainability and trust. Finally, in Sect. 5, we trace the implications of our account, and explore a few theoretical and practical workarounds.

