2  AI Systems in Decision-Making Contexts

As a general technological category, artificial intelligence – “AI” – has been instantiated across a variety of domains including medicine, business, and government; and in configurations that encompass robotic aids, virtual chatbots, and search and recommendation systems, to name but a few. In this paper, we focus on one type of AI system, specifically, AI-based Decision Support Tools – hereafter “AI-DSTs” – that output recommendations intended to support human decision-making, often as part of an integrated human-AI workflow.
AI-DSTs are, in our view, emblematic examples of close human-AI collaboration. Their implementation can be found in clinical settings for optimizing diagnosis and pharmaceutical treatment plans, in organizations to streamline hiring decisions by managers, and in government to help decision-makers craft policy using large amounts of data, to name but a few. Typically, AI-DSTs’ recommendations are received by users – sometimes called “humans-in-the-loop” (Zanzotto, 2019) – who then accept or reject the proposed action as a contribution to the decision-making workflow. The success of these final decisions therefore turns on the ability of this decision-maker to determine the suitability of the AI-DST’s recommendation against a combination of their own expertise, experience, and judgement.
In return, decision-makers expect AI-DSTs to make reasonable inferences from large amounts of data in such a way that complements their workflow. Unreasonable expectations of an AI-DST’s capabilities and limits can lead to these tools adding to the decision-makers’ cognitive burdens, instead of lessening them. To ensure that these human-AI interactions are successful in improving decision-making workflows, scholars and practitioners have surfaced various proposals for overcoming key problems that could hinder such interactions. The calls for explainability and trust are two especially prominent examples of such proposals (Floridi et al., 2018; Hagendorff, 2020).
First, the call to make AI-DSTs explainable may be seen as responding to worries about the “opaqueness” of these systems that might prevent decision-makers from understanding how their AI-DSTs work, and in turn, from effectively collaborating with them. AI-DSTs can be opaque – that is, the internal logic by which they process inputs to generate outputs can be unknown to decision-makers – for various reasons. A few such reasons are common to most decision-support technologies: sometimes the decision-maker might not have the requisite technical literacy to understand how the system works, or sometimes those who developed and deployed the system might intentionally keep its inner logic secret (Pasquale, 2015). Nevertheless, a certain kind of opacity seems unique to AI systems, one that arises out of a “mismatch between mathematical optimization in high-dimensionality characteristic of machine learning, and the demands of human-scale reasoning and styles of semantic interpretation” (Burrell, 2016, p. 2).2 Some AI-DSTs are “black-boxes” precisely because they are
