Second, the call for trust in AI may be seen as responding to worries that decisionmakers could prematurely discount their AI-DST’s recommendations, and in turn, fail to fully integrate these systems into their decision-making processes. Organizations typically need to expend considerable resources and effort to implement and integrate AI-DSTs – or indeed, any decision-support tool – into decision-making workflows. Consequently, organizations are strongly motivated to ensure that decision-makers fully utilize their AI-DSTs, to offload some of their cognitive burdens and decisionmaking tasks so that they might make better, if not more efficient decisions. However, if decision-makers do not trust their AI-DST, such benefits are unlikely to be realized. In line with these pragmatic considerations, conversations about trust in AI-DSTs often center on considerations such as ease of use, system performance and reliability, and other such factors that might affect the quality of human-AI collaboration.
2.1  Popular Calls for Explainable and Trusted AI

These two proposals have, over time, come to be connected to larger moral and practical questions surfaced in scholarship on ethical and socially-beneficial implementations of AI-DSTs. Grappling with problems of explainability and trust not only furthers the effectiveness of human-AI collaboration, but also ties into larger questions of how AI-supported decision-making can be made more fair, accountable, robust, and legitimate. As such, the rest of this section expands on the importance of explainability and trust as desirable principles to pursue in developing and deploying AI-DSTs.
2.1.1  Unpacking the Calls for Explainable AI-DSTs

Calls for explainability ask that the inner logic by which AI-DSTs process inputs to generate outputs should be made available and understandable to human decisionmakers. Computer scientists and engineers have responded enthusiastically to these calls. In recent years, AI and human-computer interaction research has seen considerable progress in the development of various explainability techniques to make the inner logic of AI systems accessible to human users. Various such explainability techniques can be used to present – through text, visualizations, graphs, networks, and other diagrams – information about an AI-DST’s features, weights, probabilities, and other relevant parameters, through which a decision-maker can come to at least a basic understanding of the system’s inner logic (for a recent review of XAI techniques, cf. Mueller et al., 2019). Other approaches – such as the provision of “counterfactual” or “contrastive” explanations (Nguyen et al., 2015; Wachter et al., 2017) – aim to provide such knowledge without opening the “black-box”, by providing counterexamples of how the system would have behaved under different scenarios.

