Of course, there remains debate over whether we can truly achieve perfect knowledge about what exactly is going on inside an AI system (Humphreys, 2004; Lipton, 2019), whether explainability necessarily comes at the cost of accuracy (Gunning, 2017; Rudin, 2019), and whether it is possible to meaningfully intervene to prevent potentially harmful decisions simply by looking at the system’s explanations (Cappelen & Dever, 2021; Robbins, 2019). Moreover, despite the availability of several explainability techniques, organizations have been slow to implement these techniques in their AI-DST deployments (Benbya et al., 2020; Deloitte, 2021). These debates and problems notwithstanding, with growing research on the explainability and interpretability of AI, decision-makers today have available to them several technical possibilities of understanding, to some extent, how their AI-DST came to its recommendations.
This brings us to a question of key importance for our paper: what exactly do we gain, morally speaking, by making AI-DSTs explainable? One overwhelmingly popular answer to this question is the following: explanations provide an epistemic warrant for trust (Pieters, 2011; von Eschenbach, 2021; Ferrario et al., 2022).3 Knowledge about the inner workings of their AI-DST, it is argued, can help decision-makers come to reasoned and well-calibrated judgments about when and why to trust its recommendations. Rather than making their own inferences and predictions about how their system might behave, decision-makers can use explanations to identify the patterns that distinguish AI-DST’s correct and incorrect outputs, and in turn, come to better-formed judgments about how to integrate these tools into their decision-making (Simon, 2010; Jacovi et al., 2021). Along these lines, explanations might be framed as reasonably acceptable if their fidelity and clarity provide sufficient evidence supporting a past recommendation, so that decision-makers come to trust future recommendations.
There remain lively debates about precisely how much, and what kinds of information needs to be communicated about an AI-DST’s inner logic to instill in a decision-maker the right kind of trust: i.e., trust in future outputs (Durán & Formanek, 2018). However, few question the straightforward manner by which explainability and trust in AI-DSTs seem to be connected to one another. Ostensibly, explanations can lead to trust, and in turn, trust can be treated as a construct that measures the effectiveness of rendered explanations.

3  Ferrario et al. (2022) provide an especially detailed and careful account of how explainability contributes to trust in AI. Specifically, they argue that explainability fosters trust if and only if it (a) provides justification for a belief about the trustworthiness of the AI system, and (b) causally contributes to rely on the AI in the absence of monitoring. Interestingly, in developing their arguments, they employ a concept of “paradigmatic trust”, one component of which is an “anti-monitoring” stance towards the trustee. As we will subsequently discuss, this view relates very closely how trust is conceptualized in the present paper (i.e., as comprising an ‘unquestioning attitude’). For this reason, one may reasonably see our paper as a natural extension to Ferrario et al.’s arguments – while they focus on the attitudes, beliefs and dispositions that make explanations useful for fostering trust in AI, we focus on what happens (at the attitudinal level) after this trust is fostered. Specifically, they ask: when does (and doesn’t) explainability foster trust? And, we ask: can explanations still serve their intended normative purpose after trust is fostered? Our thanks to an anonymous reviewer for pointing out this interesting connection.
