However, there are other popular, and deeply important answers to the question of how explanations from AI-DSTs improve decision-making.4 For one, explanations can help decision-makers perform regular “sanity checks” to ensure that their AI-DST is functioning as intended, and that its internal logic is generalizable and robust (Ribeiro et al., 2016). In case the AI system performs inconsistently and/or inaccurately, scrutinizing explanations can help the decision-maker carefully evaluate the overall capability of their AI-DST to render appropriate recommendations. Moreover, explanations might also help decision-makers identify biases in algorithmic- decisions: to detect and intervene in systems that unfairly harm (or privilege) certain groups over others (Sonboli et al., 2021). Yet another popular and important proposal suggests that explanations are important for decision-makers to preserve “meaningful control” over AI systems, so that the responsibility for AI-assisted decisions can be retained in human hands (Coeckelbergh, 2020). There are also important epistemic reasons for making AI-DSTs explainable. By analyzing how complex AI models make decisions, decision-makers may be exposed to new – and perhaps more efficient and reasonable – decision-making strategies, which they might subsequently incorporate into their own decision processes (cf. Robbins, 2019). For such reasons, despite some open questions about why exactly explainable AI matters morally, it remains relatively uncontested that, when we insist that AI-DSTs should be made explainable, decision-makers are better placed to pursue decisions that accord with fairness, meaningful human control, robustness, efficiency, and various other normatively desirable goals.
2.1.2  Unpacking the Calls for Trust in AI

Popular calls for trust in AI have often taken care to acknowledge that only certain AI systems deserve to be trusted. The fact that one is far more likely to encounter calls for ‘trustworthy AI’ than ‘trusted AI’ is a testament to this carefulness: scholars and policymakers alike have been warning against encouraging trust in AI systems that are biased, harmful, or otherwise undeserving of trust (Floridi, 2019; Chatila et al., 2021), and/or that decision-makers might place too much weight on AI-DST’s recommendations, even when they ought not to do so (cf. Cummings, 2017). Yet, at least in some cases – when AI-DSTs are carefully designed to be lawful, ethical, and robust (European Commission, 2019), when decision-makers are unduly biased against accepting their AI-DST’s recommendations (Dietvorst et al., 2015), or when the decision-making task at hand requires certain data-analysis and prediction tasks that an AI system could perform much more efficiently than a human decision-maker (cf. Killock, 2020; Sreshta et al., 2019) – it seems appropriate to call on decisionmakers to take their AI-DST’s recommendations more seriously. In such cases, calls for trust in AI seem reasonable.

