However, it is difficult to pin down what exactly this ‘trust’ entails. This is in part because the concept itself is continuously unsettled and contested: trust has been theorized in a variety of intellectual traditions, each with their own unique understanding of what the concept entails. It falls outside the scope of this paper to engage in any substantive debates about the nature of trust. However, to provide some scaffolding for our subsequent analysis, it is useful here to briefly describe important distinctions between different approaches to theorizing trust.
Some views of trust are morally-loaded, in that they aim to characterize how a trustor ought to evaluate the trustworthiness of potential trustees, and/or how trustees ought to respond to the trust that is placed in them. On some such views, the typical trustor should trust someone when they observe that this potential trustee possesses certain desirable properties that make them trustworthy – benevolence, integrity, reliability, honor, etc. (Hoff & Bashir, 2015; Mayer et al., 1995). Other views suggest that a trustee should take the fact that they are being trusted to do X as a reason to do X, and correspondingly, their trustor can take this expectation of goodwill as a reason to trust them (Jones, 1996). Importantly, most moral views of trust are agentdirected. That is, moral trust usually involves the ascription of certain agential states and/or properties to both trustors and trustees, that only human agents can be said to reasonably possess (Nickel, 2010). This makes such views relatively unhelpful for characterizing trust in AI, since non-agential technological artifacts cannot reasonably be described as having intentions, consistently adhering to principles, or being motivated by goodwill towards trustors. As such, if artifacts cannot possess any of the properties that trust, in a morally-loaded sense of the term, is based on, then ‘trust’ in an AI system is either mistaken (in that it ascribes to the system agential properties that it does not possess), or misdirected (in that it confuses trust in the system’s human developers and deployers for trust in the system itself). As Ryan (2020) persuasively puts it, “AI should not be viewed as trustworthy because [this] undermines the value of interpersonal trust, anthropomorphizes AI […] and diverts responsibility from those developing and using AI” (p. 2750).5
However, it is also possible to conceptualize trust at a descriptive level. Here, the emphasis is on accurately and completely characterizing the attitudes and behaviors that the typical trustor adopts towards a potential trustee. As such, descriptive characterizations of human-AI trust would focus on describing the attitudes that a human decision-maker might adopt towards an AI-DST that they trust, and the factors that influence these trust-based interactions. There is a rich body of empirical literature - especially in the fields of psychology and human-computer interaction – that attempt to answer such questions (cf. Lee & See, 2004). Here, trust has been found to variously depend on dispositional factors (such as the personality or age)

