However, it is important to note that most empirical research on this topic conceptualizes ‘trust’ as a matter of adjusting one’s reliance on a system such that one tends to accept correct recommendations and reject incorrect recommendations (Ehsan et al., 2021; Hoff & Bashir, 2015). On this widely-received view, trust in technology is a matter of “appropriate reliance” (Lee & See, 2004) where “the basis of trust [is described] in terms of the performance of an agent, the trustee, who furthers the goals of an individual, the trustor” (p. 54). In our opinion, however, such purely goal-oriented views do not always accord with reasonable intuitions about what trust in AI-DSTs in fact looks like. If, for instance, a decision-maker constantly monitors their AI-DST and expends considerable cognitive effort to painstakingly scrutinize its output and explanations for each decision, we would be inclined to think that they do not trust their AI-DST – even if they end up accepting most of its correct recommendations and rejecting most of its incorrect ones. If this inclination is correct, we ought to prefer a view of trust that describes a trustor’s willingness to rely on a trustee without (or with relatively little) monitoring, suspicion, and scrutiny (Taddeo, 2017).
To clarify this discrepancy, it is useful to tap into a widely-accepted philosophical distinction between ‘trust’ and ‘mere reliance’. Baier (1986), who first popularized this distinction, offers the following heuristic to help us separate the two concepts. When we rely on someone to do X, and they fail to do X, we feel at most disappointed. In this case, we have no serious grounds to be personally, sharply affected by this failure. However, when we trust someone to do X, and they fail to do X, we feel betrayed. Trust, being more normatively loaded than reliance, contains the possibility of normative failure, and hence betrayal. As such, to fully describe the attitudinal orientation that some trustor (say, some decision-maker) adopts towards a trustee (their AI-DST), we must be able to account for the possibility of betrayal – and its accompanying sharp emotional response – in cases of failure.6

6  Baier ultimately defends a morally-loaded view of trust: she believes that a trustor’s assumption of goodwill on part of their trustee is what generates betrayal during failures, and in turn, is what separates trust from mere reliance. As mentioned before, we wish to keep the arguments in this paper neutral between specific conceptualizations of trust – since our focus is on characterizing the attitudes that the typical trustor adopts towards a trustee. As such, we are taking Baier’s distinction between trust (betrayal) and reliance (disappointment) as an attitudinal marker to separate the two concepts in terms of the differing attitudes they evoke in trustors (or reliers). This usage of Baier’s distinction is not especially idiosyncratic – Holton (1994) is a notable example of an attempt to theorize trust using Baier’s trust-reliance distinction as an attitudinal marker.
