Is it possible to conceptualize trust in AI at a descriptive level while retaining a sensitivity to the trust-reliance distinction? We believe so. To generate this intuition, consider the following scenario. Suppose a laptop, one that has been in our possession for several years and functioned perfectly throughout this period, suddenly fails in the middle of drafting a manuscript. At this critical moment of failure, it seems like an understatement to say that we would feel merely disappointed. Rather, we might reasonably experience a sharp emotional response towards the laptop – perhaps anger of a sort that gestures towards betrayal. Our “trust” – if this is indeed the accurate term – does not seem to be tied to any beliefs about the laptop’s agential properties (to its “benevolence” or “integrity”): it does not seem like we are necessarily anthropomorphizing the laptop. Our trust, further, need not necessarily be tied to any normative expectations from the developers, manufacturers, and various other human agents that were involved in building the laptop and bringing it in contact with us. Rather, our feelings of ‘betrayal’ might be more reliably traced to the longstanding positive cooperative relationship we built with this laptop over years, and our well-founded (but ultimately frustrated) expectation that it would reliably support our manuscript-drafting efforts. We feel betrayed, to borrow Margalit’s (2017) language, when our thick relations with the laptop come unglued (p. 47), and we no longer feel confident depending on it for future writing tasks.
It is not uncommon to encounter such descriptions of thick (intimate, affective) relations between humans and technical artifacts. Empirical studies in human-robot interaction, for instance, have documented the ways in which we form deep attachments with robots, as, over sustained positive interactions, we gradually come to see them as reasonable targets for intimacy, affection, and strong normative expectations (see e.g. Sung et al., 2007; Darling et al., 2015; Mollen et al., 2022). Studies have also documented people ascribing intentionality and other such agential attributes to technical artifacts that they interact with (see e.g., Wiese et al., 2017; Bigman et al., 2019).7 Betrayal – in the descriptively rich sense of the emotion – has also been studied in human- technology interactions, especially in cases where technologies deceive us about what they can and cannot do (see e.g., Danaher et al., 2020; Saetra, 2021). In such cases, as Coecklebergh (2012) argues, it does not matter so much whether robots (or other seemingly ‘intelligent’ technical artifacts, like AI-DSTs) are legitimate moral agents – what matters is that they appear to us as more than objects. It is therefore not unreasonable to imagine that a decision-maker might adopt attitudes and behaviors towards an AI-DST they trust that very closely resemble the paradigmatic case of human-human trust. Importantly this ‘trust’ – in a descriptively rich, but non-normative sense of the term – would entail the adoption of quite differ-

