In Sect. 4.2., we will return to develop a more complete view of what such ‘trust’ between a decision-maker and their AI-DST might look like, and why this trust is at least sometimes desirable. For the purposes of this section, however, it should suffice to appreciate that trust and reliance entail quite different attitudinal states, and, whether or not we can ‘trust’ AI in the same morally-laden way as we would another person, it is at least plausible to think that we could reasonably adopt trust-like attitudes and behaviors towards AI systems in ways that go beyond mere reliance.

3  A Process Model Between Explainability and Trust
Beyond AI-DSTs and AI systems more generally, scholars – especially in the fields of information systems and organizational studies – have proposed various models that aim to explain and predict the effective integration of new workplace technologies. Many of these proposals build upon the prominent “Technology Acceptance Model” – or “TAM” – first advanced by Davis (1989), in which he proposes and validates two theoretical constructs, perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use, to explain both current and predicted use and acceptance of information systems. According to TAM, perceived usefulness is an important factor since “people tend to use or not use an application to the extent they believe it will help them perform their job better” (Davis, 1989, p. 320). In turn, even if a new technology might be seen as beneficial to future job performance, understanding its perceived ease of use is important since technology rejection may arise from users who “believe that the [system] is too hard to use and that the performance benefits of usage are outweighed by the effort of using the application” (p. 320). Subsequently, Venkatesh and Davis’ (2000) extended model, TAM2, incorporates additional theoretical constructs to include “social influence processes (subjective norm, voluntariness, and image) and cognitive instrumental processes (job relevance, output quality, result demonstrability, and perceived ease of use)” (Venkatesh & Davis, 2000, p. 187). Substantial evidence has been presented in broad support of TAM, and its influence as a process model that aims to identify and measure factors in pursuit of a clearly defined end-goal – i.e., technology acceptance – has continued to pervade industry practice and academic scholarship since.
Current industry language around AI-DSTs that describes the basic instrumentality of explainability building trust is strikingly similar to descriptions of TAM and related process models. The need for explainability, in this case, is described as helping users calibrate their trust: to be able to know when to “double-check” the recommendations of AI-DSTs, to speed up user onboarding and system familiarity/confidence, and to manage expectations around building user understanding of inscrutable systems. In turn, these calibrations help to further manage the overall influence that AI-DSTs might have on users (Chatila et al., 2021; Floridi, 2019;
