3.1  Explanations Building Trust; Trust Leading into Adoption

There has been considerable recent research to better understand how explanations build – and sometimes fail to build – trust. Explanations that do not represent the system’s inner logic with enough detail can be unduly disregarded (Papenmeier et al., 2019; Shin, 2021; Weitz et al., 2019), but so too can explanations that are overly complex and hence difficult to understand (Bansal et al., 2021; Yang et al., 2017). Decision-makers that have contextual and domain-specific expertise can be more likely to interpret their AI-DSTs explanations accurately, and hence produce correct decisions (Alufaisan et al., 2020; Janssen et al., 2020). Conversely, this would also imply that explanations should be tailored to prioritize certain context- or domainspecific information to complement their users. Yet, the presence of tailored explanations or interpretability tools might also be taken for granted – that is, the superficial presence of explanations might stand in for actual scrutiny and deliberation over the substantive content of explanations themselves – which could, in turn, lead to cases of over-trust and misuse (Kaur et al., 2020). These findings, in general, suggest that although explanations can be useful for decision-makers to build trust, the link between the two principles should not be taken to be unproblematic.
Moreover, prior research in the fields of information systems and organizational behavior has also shown that trust in decision-support tools tends to drive positive perceptions about their ease of use and usability (Wu et al., 2011). Similar refrains can be commonly heard in contemporary conversations about trust in AI-DSTs: about how the potential benefits and returns of implementing AI systems will fail to be realized if decision-makers do not trust – and in turn, accept and adopt – their AIDSTs (Chatila et al., 2021; Gillespie et al., 2020; Stanton & Jensen, 2021). Trust in AI is therefore suggested to be crucial for ensuring that decision-makers fully adopt AI-DSTs into their decision-making processes, and effectively integrate these tools into their decision-making workflows. Explainability, insofar as it leads to trust, is ostensibly desirable for the same reason.
This model of human-AI interaction, which largely follows a similar procedural logic to TAM, seems to be an attractive proposition for thinking through and identifying the factors which might hinder the adoption and acceptance of AI-DSTs. Moreover, the theoretical constructs that such an approach provides are clear ways of operationalizing and measuring human and organizational factors that can be practically addressed and acted upon. In this sense, the utility of the process model should not be discounted. Our contention, however, is that the normative value of these two principles extends far beyond their linear relationship to one another, and in turn, to adoption. On the process model, once explanations serve their intended purpose of building trust, they ostensibly cease to be of value. This view, as we discussed previously, seems rather incomplete: we care about explainability for a variety of important moral goals that do not diminish in importance even after trust is achieved. The inadequacy of the process model to take into account these rich and diverse moral

