4  Attitudinal States for Explainable and Trusted Ai
Consider the following vignette: Zoe is using an AI-DST to assist in prescribing medicines to her patients. This
system outputs a list of drugs and their prescribed dosing amounts, based on the patient’s characteristics and Zoe’s diagnosis, among other inputs. Zoe has not been using this system for very long, and does not yet have a good understanding of its strengths and weaknesses. Fortunately, the AI-DST happens to be explainable. She deliberates over each outputted prediction and explanation with great care, and over time, comes to a well-calibrated understanding of when the system tends to get things right and wrong. Over time, she comes to trust that, at least for a certain set of medical diagnoses, the AI-DST is particularly good at prescribing the correct medicines and doses. As a result, when dealing with the diagnoses in this trusted set, she ceases to be suspicious about the AI-DST’s reliability and begins to accept its prescriptions without much scrutiny or deliberation. If, after this point, the AI-DST happens to output a gravely erroneous prescription for a diagnosis in the trusted set, Zoe would be unlikely to notice this mistake.
This vignette, while perhaps a little simplistic, illustrates a tension that we will develop for the rest of this section. Zoe’s trust in her AI system seems thoughtful and well-calibrated, based on her careful deliberation about its output and explanations. But, when this trust is established, she begins to ignore its explanations, and in turn, misses out on potentially vital information about its biases and errors. This tension, we suggest, is not some accidental feature of our imagined vignette: rather, it inevitably emerges when we pursue normatively full-blooded conceptions of what it means to trust an AI system, and why we ought to make AI explainable.
4.1  Explainability and the Deliberative Attitude
In Sect. 2.1 above, we briefly outlined various morally desirable goals that an AIDST’s explanations can help us pursue: fairness, trust, legitimacy, and accountability, to name a few. Here, we wish to once again reiterate that explanations might in some cases be necessary, but never sufficient for achieving these moral goals. The mere act of making an AI system explainable does not, by itself, guarantee us desirable ethical outcomes. Rather, explanations carry information about an AI system that needs to be scrutinized, reasoned about, and acted on in ways that further moral ends.
Minimally, then, a decision-maker should pay close attention to their AI-DST’s explanations: they should carefully monitor their system and proactively engage with the content of its explanations during the decision-making process. It would not suffice, for instance, to take for granted the mere fact that their system is explainable, and only retrospectively review its explanations after something goes wrong. Rather,
