decision-makers should proactively look inside the black-box, so to speak, and incorporate the information contained in their AI-DST’s explanations beyond being content with its superficial presence. By way of highlighting the need for such proactive monitoring, consider the popular proposal that explanations are necessary for decision-makers to retain meaningful control over (and in turn, be held accountable for) AI-supported decisions. For control to be meaningful, at minimum, decision-makers must have sufficient information about how the decision in question was made, including about the tools that supported the decision-making process (Coeckelbergh, 2020). This epistemic condition for meaningful control would clearly be unmet in cases where the decision-maker failed to monitor their AI-DST and the information contained in its explanations. As such, insofar as we care about decision-makers retaining meaningful control over all AI-supported decisions, it does not seem warranted for them to, at any point, stop monitoring the system’s explanations.
However, it is also unhelpful to uncritically assume that knowing comes from merely looking (Ananny & Crawford, 2018). For a decision-maker to come to a well-reasoned assessment about whether to accept an AI-DST’s recommendation, they must critically reflect about what its explanations imply in relation to broader social, political, and technical contexts surrounding the system’s design and implementation. Decision-makers, then, should not merely “look into” their AI-DST, but also look across them – “[to see] them as sociotechnical systems that do not contain complexity but enact complexity by connecting to and intertwining with assemblages of humans and non-humans” (Ananny & Crawford, 2018, p. 982). Therefore, for a decision-maker to spot biases in their AI-DSTs, they must reflexively connect information contained in its explanations – about the system’s hidden and immaterial layers of design – to broader social and economic structures, and in turn, to the injustice, oppression, and exploitation generated within these structures (Hollanek, 2020). It would follow, then, that critical reflection should also be an important aspect of how decision-makers interact with their AI-DSTs.
The particular mode described above – of interacting with an AI-DST through proactive monitoring and critical reflection – is what we are calling here a “deliberative attitude”. The need for a deliberative attitude emerges in response to the opacity that characterizes AI-supported decision-making, and as such, seem to be more demanding and rigorous – in terms of both frequency and cognitive complexity – than ordinary calls to conduct routine “audits” and periodic “maintenance checks” of workplace tools (Clark et al., 1988; Grint & Woolgar, 2013). Indeed, different types of AI systems require different kinds of scrutiny depending on the underlying model architecture (cf. Arrieta et al., 2020), and there are a variety of informational cues – model parameters, intended use, evaluation data, training data, performance metrics, etc. – that a proactive ‘human in the loop’ would need to carefully scrutinize in their interactions with an AI-DST (cf. Mitchell et al., 2019). For this reason, we suggest, to ensure that the explanations from AI-DSTs meaningfully serve the pursuit of a broad range of normatively desirable goals beyond trust, it is crucial for decision-makers to adopt and maintain a deliberative attitude towards their AI-DSTs.

