4.2  Trust and the Unquestioning Attitude
In Sect. 2.2 above, we highlighted a widely-accepted distinction between trust and mere reliance, and outlined some of the difficulties with conceptualizing what it means to trust – in a descriptively rich sense of the term – artifacts. In light of this distinction and its accompanying difficulties, we need to conceptualize trust in a way that at once preserves the trust-reliance distinction, while also allowing for trust in artifacts. A recent – and in our view, rather convincing – proposal by Nguyen (2020) might help us do so.
Broadly, Nguyen accepts Baier’s distinction between trust and reliance, and her attitudinal heuristic for separating the two, but rejects the view that trust can only be directed towards agents. To build his case, he invites us to consider what we would feel like if we were using a sturdy-looking rope to scale up a wall, and halfway through our journey, the rope suddenly breaks. Suppose, prior to this climb, we had carefully inspected the rope and found it to be of high quality, and that we had no reason to doubt that it was fit for purpose prior to its breaking. In this case, to say that we would feel merely disappointed when the rope breaks seems like an understatement. Indeed, the sharp shock and distress seems closer to some form of betrayal. Nguyen thinks this is because when we trust the rope, we are not bothering to consider whether it would hold steady (or fail) – we simply use it for our purposes while focusing our cognitive resources on other matters (p. 9). Trust, in this sense, is an “unquestioning attitude” – it emerges when we put questions about the rope’s reliability out of mind. Conversely, if we were to distrust the rope, we would climb carefully and re-examine it closely at every step. In this case, we might still be (forced to) rely on the rope, but “[this] reliance is tentative, and demands constant reassurance” (p. 9). If the rope were to give way now, we might feel disappointed, but not betrayed. In this way, Nguyen thinks we can preserve Baier’s distinction between trust and mere reliance even when talking about objects that are clearly non-agential.9
Although Nguyen’s account is not explicitly focused on AI systems, his view can help us understand what it would mean for a decision-maker to have full-blooded trust in their AI-DST. When a decision-maker adopts an unquestioning attitude towards their AI-DST – when they are not disposed to actively interrogate its reliability, and do not suspect that the system will unpredictably change its behavior – they trust the system.10 This does not entail that they will never question the system at all,

