4.3  Attitudinal Tensions in the Joint Pursuit of Explainable and Trusted AI

As we have seen, for the explanations of an AI-DST to serve their intended normative ends, a decision-maker must adopt a deliberative attitude. And, for a decision-maker to trust their AI-DST in a descriptively rich sense of the term, they must adopt an unquestioning attitude. Although we have previously hinted at deep tensions between these two attitudes, we now make these tensions explicit. To do so, let us consider some simple – albeit extreme – scenarios.
Consider a decision-maker who, at some time t1 has a high level of trust in their AIDST. This trust was established prior to t1 and could have been done so in a variety of ways: perhaps, as is often the case, they were directly and closely in the development process of this AI-DST and over time, they have come to believe that the system is especially well-tailored to augmenting their decision-making process.12 Or, simply, perhaps their previous interactions with this AI-DST have been overwhelmingly positive. For whatever reason, from t1 onwards, the decision-maker adopts and maintains an unquestioning attitude towards their AI-DST. This entails that they put questions about the AI-DSTs reliability out of mind and are not disposed to actively monitor and scrutinize its output or explanations before accepting its recommendations. After t1, they do not actively suspect that the system’s recommendations could be biased or otherwise erroneous. However, active monitoring, scrutiny, and critical reflection are all crucial aspects of the deliberative attitude that is necessary for explainable AI to be morally useful. As we have argued above, for the system’s explanations to meaningfully serve towards their intended moral goals, the decision-maker must actively monitor the content of these explanations, remain suspicious about the possibility of biases and other errors creeping in at any time, and maintain a careful sensitivity to the social, technical, and political context surrounding the AI system. Their unquestioning attitude makes such scrutiny unfeasible. The decision-maker’s trust in the AI system disposes them to gloss over the complex and nuanced information contained in the content of its explanations, or perhaps even ignore these explanations entirely. As such, after t1, the normative value of the system’s explanations for making AIsupported decisions fair, accountable and legitimate appears greatly diminished.13

12  For an AI-DST to be deployed in consequential decision-making processes, developmental milestones that have to be met often involve the negotiation of expertise between domain- and developer-knowledge. As recent ethnographic work in organizational studies indicates, these processes can be long and drawnout, including both significant model compromise and extension (Kim & Mehrizi, 2022; Kim et al., 2022). It is, in our view, entirely feasible for individuals like Zoe to be involved in the development process through the translation of her clinical experience and expertise into model parameters. Resultantly, if Zoe believes that the resulting AI-DST is well-tailored to her needs and concerns, she is more likely to retain an unquestioning attitude in her interactions with this AI-DST that she has had significant involvement in training and deploying. 13  This is not to say, of course, that explanations are morally irrelevant at this point. For example, in case of harmful system malfunctions, explainability can be a valuable tool for retrospectively tracking how these malfunctions occurred, and how future similar malfunctions might be avoided. As such, even in cases where decision-makers no longer effectively scrutinizing their AI-DST’s explanations (and in turn, failing to meaningfully perform their roles as ‘humans-in-the-loop’), these explanations can still be morally useful in other ways. Our thanks to an anonymous reviewer for pointing out the need for this important clarification.
