It is important to note that our argument is agnostic about how decision-makers come to trust their AI-DST (i.e., what happens before t1). For this reason, we do not necessarily contradict the various empirical findings we discussed in Sect. 3 that show how explanations can serve to build trust in AI systems. Suppose instead, at some t0, the decision-maker is distrustful of their AI-DST, but upon carefully scrutinizing its explanations, they find its internal logic to be sufficiently robust, which then serves as a warrant for their trust. Over time, through continued interactions and scrutiny, they reach a state of high trust at t1. That is, over time they no longer find it necessary to maintain continuous scrutiny of the system’s explanations: they take their previous successful interactions with their AI-DST’s output and explanations as inductive support for the system’s continued trustworthiness. At t1, then, they adopt an unquestioning attitude towards their AI-DST and start to spend less of their finite cognitive resources to question its reliability – perhaps choosing to be content with the mere existence of future explanations as a signal to reinforce their earlier decision to trust their AI-DST. At this point, once trust is stably secured, the normative value of the system’s explanations once again diminishes: these explanations once again cease to play a meaningful role in making AI-supported decisions fair, accountable and legitimate. For this reason, even when explanations provide the basis for trust in AI-DSTs, the tensions between the unquestioning and deliberative attitudes persist.
Finally, let us return to Zoe from our earlier vignette. We know that she started off at a low-trust state at t0. She carefully scrutinized the system’s output and explanations and learned that in some cases – i.e., when the AI-DST was prescribing medicines for a certain set of diagnoses – the system tended to be correct, and outside these cases, the system tended to get things wrong. Her scrutiny, therefore, did not provide her with a warrant for unqualified trust in their AI-DST. Rather, over time, as she gained a better understanding of the system’s successes and failures, her trust came to be finely calibrated. In certain cases, where Zoe expected the AI-DST to perform well, she unquestioningly accepted its output, and in all other cases, she carefully deliberated about its output and explanations. By switching between unquestioning and deliberative attitudes depending on the situation, it might seem like Zoe is able to avoid the attitudinal tensions discussed above.
Not quite, we think. Trust in an AI-DST, even when finely calibrated, can still be in tension with the normative value of explanations. For one, AI-supported decision-making processes are typically dynamic: AI-DSTs often train and update their parameters continuously with new data, and the contextual factors surrounding a decision can also change in significant ways (Gama et al., 2014; Tsymbal, 2004). This dynamism means that, in general, AI-DSTs are unstable objects of trust – prior evidence about their reliability within certain domains may not be fully indicative of their future performance. New virus mutations, for example, could make Zoe’s AI-DST less effective in its antiviral prescriptions, or a new update to her system might introduce a bias that did not exist previously. In such cases, Zoe’s previously calibrated trust may no longer be warranted, and she would have to once again monitor and critically reflect about the system’s output and explanations to recalibrate her trust. The more dynamic the decision-making context in question, the more often one would have to perform such recalibrations: but continuous recalibration requires the

