continuous adoption of a deliberative attitude, which itself forecloses full-blooded trust.
Moreover, even when trust is finely calibrated, there remain difficult questions about holding decision-makers responsible for decisions that they have accepted unquestioningly. Suppose Zoe’s AI-DST outputs a prescription for a certain medical diagnosis, and Zoe, based on her previous scrutiny, trusts that the system performs especially well when faced with this particular diagnosis. Unfortunately, there happens to be a rare failure: the decision is erroneous, possibly in a deeply harmful way. Oblivious to this fact, Zoe accepts the system’s decision unquestioningly, without scrutinizing its explanations – and in turn, fails to prevent the ensuing harms. Now, one possible response to this case might be to suggest that Zoe’s conduct was, all things considered, appropriate. Her trust was finely calibrated based on her careful previous scrutiny of the system and, despite this rare failure, her calibrated trust would allow her to efficiently produce correct decisions most of the time. But, we suspect, such a response would be deeply dissatisfying to those who would want to hold Zoe responsible for this particular erroneous decision’s harms. The AI-DST’s explanations should have provided her with enough information so that she could intervene in time to prevent its harmful decisions. Her decision to stop scrutinizing these explanations, and in turn, her failure to stop its harmful decision, seems to be reasonable grounds for holding her – as the so-called human-in-the-loop – accountable for the ensuring harms. As such, for those who argue for XAI on the basis that explanations are necessary for holding decision-makers responsible for harmful decisions, full-blooded trust – calibrated or otherwise – would seem deeply inappropriate.
With these illustrations, we are trying to show that it is profoundly difficult for any decision-maker to both trust their AI-DST while also responding in an appropriate way – through proactive monitoring and critical reflection – to its explanations. Unquestioning and deliberative attitudes are incompatible attitudinal states for any decision-maker to occupy simultaneously. For an explanation to be normatively useful, it must be carefully scrutinized, evaluated, and acted on. Full-blooded trust, which entails the adoption of an unquestioning attitude towards one’s AI-DST, makes such scrutiny difficult. For this reason, the joint pursuit of explainable and trusted AI runs into an intractable implementation gap: these two principles, when put into practice, call upon decision-makers to adopt and maintain two distinct attitudes towards their AI-DST that are profoundly difficult to adopt simultaneously, and the adoption of either attitude supports the pursuit of one principle at the expense of the other. By sticking to the process model – where the two principles are defined only in terms of their instrumental value for increasing technology adoption and acceptance – we would likely fail to uncover these attitudinal tensions between explainability and trust. However, by adopting a more comprehensive view of what these two principles entail, it becomes clear that their joint pursuit will encounter, and in turn, must overcome a critical implementation gap.

