5  Implications and Recommendations

At this point, it is important to clarify the scope and implications of our intended conclusion. We do not mean to suggest that the joint pursuit of explainable and trusted AI is somehow necessarily self-defeating, or that we must choose one of these principles to abandon entirely. Our two intended provocations are rather more modest. First, we suggest that the assumed straightforward connection between explainability and trust – where we care about the former only insofar as it leads to the latter – is inadequate, for it fails to account for the rich and varied normative reasons for why explainability and trust in AI matter. And secondly, we suggest that since the joint pursuit of these two principles entails the simultaneous adoption of incompatible attitudinal states, this joint pursuit is likely to be hindered by a thorny implementation gap between principles and practice.
Note, however, that this gap arises out of our theoretical conception of (a) why we care about the explainability of AI, and (b) what it means to trust AI in a descriptively rich sense. Of course, it is wholly possible to conceptualize trust and explainability in a different way, to avoid these attitudinal tensions altogether. For instance, alternative justifications for explainability might focus primarily on how explanations can benefit decision subjects rather than decision-makers. Such justifications might foreground the oft-discussed ‘right to explanation’: where, at least on certain readings of the European Union’s General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), decisionsubjects have the right to obtain meaningful information about the logic, significance, and expected consequences of an AI-supported decision (Edwards & Veale, 2017; Goodman & Flaxman, 2017). Vredenburgh (2019), for instance, suggests that such a right might be grounded in the requirements of fairness, and particularly in the requirement for decision subjects to be able to engage in informed self-advocacy . Similar proposals focus on the decision subject’s ability to contest a decision, seek recourse for unfair outcomes, and give meaningfully informed consent to institutions that employ AI-DSTs (Edwards & Veale, 2017; Kaminski, 2021; Kim & Routledge, 2021; Ustun et al., 2019). If the moral reasons for pursuing XAI solely focus on the decision-subject, we might no longer need decision-makers to adopt and maintain the deliberative attitude, and in turn, pursue full-blooded trust without running into the implementation gap discussed above. Such a move might seem especially attractive to those who think that the cognitive burdens of scrutinizing explanations and being an effective ‘human in the loop’ are too demanding. But in turn, we might have to give up on various existing, and widely-defended, moral reasons for pursuing explainability – e.g., to hold decision-makers accountable, and to help them spot biases and errors. Given growing worries about algorithmic bias and unaccountable algorithmic decision-making, this option seems rather unattractive.
Alternatively, perhaps we could seek to conceptualize ‘trust’ in such a way that forecloses the adoption of an unquestioning attitude as a reasonable way for decisionmakers to relate to their AI-DSTs. One way to do so might be to insist on a strict, normative distinction between trust and mere reliance. Here, one may argue that trust is strictly interpersonal (i.e., between human agents), and that decision-makers may at most rely on their AI-DSTs. If only reliance is desired – and all that matters is the probability of the decision-maker accepting their AI-DST’s correct recommenda-

