tions and rejecting its incorrect ones – there is no requirement for them to maintain an unquestioning attitude towards their AI-DST. In fact, maintaining a deliberative attitude might even be conducive to effective reliance. Decision-makers that proactively monitor and critically reflect about their AI-DST’s output and explanations would be especially well-placed to make accurate decisions about when to accept or reject their system’s recommendations. Moreover, existing scholarship in the fields of management, information systems, and human-computer interaction has explored how “appropriate reliance”14 relates to the acceptance and adoption of technological systems (Wu et al., 2011). As such, insofar as we are concerned about increasing the adoption of AI-DSTs by decision-makers, perhaps a focus on reliance is sufficient. Having said that, as we have argued above, trust and reliance differ not only normatively, but also descriptively. It seems reasonable to think that decision-makers could adopt similar attitudes and dispositions towards a trusted AI as they would towards a human they trust – and at least sometimes, this ‘trust’ (in a descriptively rich, but non-normative sense of the term) might be desirable. A pressing worry is that solely focusing on reliance would entail giving up on “agential integration” – the state of synergy, as Nguyen describes it, where the decision-maker allows their AI-DST to be intimately connected to their decision-making process. It seems reasonable to think that decision-makers should do more than just rely – cautiously and tentatively – on their AI-DSTs. Rather, we hope that through rich and synergistic user-AI collaborations, we might move towards decision-making processes that make the best use of both algorithmic data processing and human judgement. It is unclear how, if at all, mere reliance can help decision-makers to pursue these desirable synergies with their AI-DSTs.
In general, we recognize that the general strategy pursued so far – of sidestepping our identified implementation gap by simply tweaking our concepts of explainability and trust – might seem rather disappointing. Still, we thought it was important to discuss, albeit briefly, what this strategy entails. In part, we wish to highlight how our preceding analysis of the attitudinal tension hinges on the – reasonable, but still contested – conceptualizations of explainability and trust that we have adopted in this paper. We also remain open to the possibility that there might be ways to conceive of trust and explainability that accord with our various intuitions about these concepts, while also avoiding the attitudinal tensions discussed above. We wish to invite future theorists to develop such concepts, and hope that our brief discussion in this section so far might help towards their theorizing.
In the remainder of this section, allow us to explore another strategy to move beyond the implementation gap in the joint pursuit of these two principles – one with more straightforwardly practical implications. At the heart of this strategy is the recognition that this gap arises from the difficulties of asking any single decisionmaker to simultaneously adopt both unquestioning and deliberative attitudes towards their AI-DST. But, in a more complex decision-making chain, it might be possible
