for different groups to maintain each of these attitudes separately. In practice, decision-making chains can be arranged so that someone (or some group of people) is explicitly charged with monitoring and critically analyzing the AI-DST’s output and explanations – i.e., by maintaining a deliberative attitude – so that others may take the fact that the system is being scrutinized as their basis for adopting an unquestioning attitude. Put differently: some deliberate, so that others can trust.
This general strategy may be implemented in various ways. For one, organizations may explicitly task the so-called “human-in-the-loop” – the person who directly interfaces with the AI-DST – to constantly scrutinize and deliberate about the system’s recommendations and explanations. These intermediaries would, in turn, pass on only those recommendations that they deem appropriate to a decision-maker, who would make the final decision. In such an arrangement, it would clearly be undesirable for the human-in-the-loop to stop maintaining the deliberative attitude at any time. However, higher-level decision-makers may, on the basis that someone has already done the work to carefully select the system’s appropriate recommendations, unquestioningly accept these selected recommendations. Similarly, this intermediating function might also be performed by a group of people – organizations may charge a so-called “red-team” with purposefully maintaining a deliberative (or perhaps even adversarial) stance towards their AI-DST and trust the system on the basis that this team’s scrutiny and deliberation is adequate and reliable. In such a way, some decision-makers might be able to enjoy the cognitive benefits of agential integration with their AI-DST, while ensuring that the system’s explanations are carefully monitored and analyzed simultaneously.
Certainly, such arrangements may not be feasible in all decision-making contexts. In clinical settings, to take one example, decision-support systems are meant to be used directly by doctors and other medical professionals, leaving little room for the meaningful involvement of any intermediary.15 Moreover, it might sometimes be prohibitively expensive and inefficient for organizations to set up internal teams exclusively to scrutinize their AI-DSTs without making any decisions themselves. In such situations, one alternate strategy – that not only represents an efficient process, but might also add additional legitimacy to the use and implementation of these tools overall – might be to outsource the responsibility of deliberation and scrutiny to external institutions.
In the automotive and aviation industry in the United States, for example, external, governmental bodies such as the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration or the Federal Aviation Administration respectively carry out research to identify current and future issues, set and harmonize safety standards, and enforce or audit internal processes. Further, these institutions, by merit of being external to and inde-

15  It is useful to note that, in some cases, AI-DSTs that impose significant liability burdens on individual decision-makers might represent a good target for regulatory interventions. This is especially the case in domains – such as clinical decision-making – that fall clearly under purview of a powerful and wellresourced regulator, who might be able to intervene in reviewing and validating AI-DSTs, and properly calibrating and demarcating the professional liability of decision-makers who use these systems. Our thanks to an anonymous reviewer for pointing out that Zoe’s AI-DST for clinical prescriptions, introduced earlier in Sect. 4.3, would be a good regulatory target, and if so, it is possible that Zoe might not be held personally liable for harms ensuing from the AI-DSTs potential errors.
